[gd_scene load_steps=56 format=3]

; Standing inside the habitat drum.
;
; Geometry arrives at runtime from tools/export_scene.py; materials come from
; godot/materials/*.tres, which station/materials.py generates. What this file
; holds is the join between them -- which material each generated group is
; bound to -- plus the environment and the tonemapper.
;
; The materials were briefly duplicated here as scene sub-resources, because
; godot/materials/ was being rebuilt at the same time as this scene. They are
; now bound by reference. Two copies of a colour table is precisely the drift
; this project keeps being bitten by, and the moment the generated set existed
; the copy had to go.
;
; THE LIGHTING PREMISE. The habitat is lit by the longitudinal light runs on
; the guideway trusses. That is authority 1 -- `Babylon_5_2-22_34b.jpg` shows
; the tubes running alongside the truss and `33a` the rectangular fixtures on
; its underside -- and it is not an axial sun strip and not the end caps. So
; the light in this scene is not in this file at all: `render_shot.gd` builds
; an OmniLight3D array from positions `export_scene.light_runs()` derives from
; the truss geometry itself. Move the truss and the light moves with it.
;
; AMBIENT IS NOT A CHEAT HERE. Outdoors a raised ambient is a way of hiding
; that there is no bounce. Inside the drum there genuinely is bounce: it is a
; closed cavity of 4.5 million m^2 with mid-albedo ground on every side, and
; every surface can see most of the others. A high ambient term is the cheap
; stand-in for interreflection that a closed cavity actually has, and turning
; it off would be the wrong answer, not the honest one.
;
; WHERE THE COLOURS CAME FROM, since the measurements were made here and the
; generated .tres files carry only the results:
;
;   sampled off Babylon_5_2-22_34b.jpg     R      G      B
;   light run tube (clipped core)          1.000  1.000  0.94-0.97
;   guideway truss lattice                 0.204  0.200  0.181
;   arable field green                     0.510  0.518  0.367
;   end cap course grey                    0.430  0.441  0.458
;   end cap outer grey (ribs)              0.321  0.327  0.350
;   core tube barrel                       0.322  0.329  0.315
;
; `33a` shows the same volume under a strong warm grade -- its nominally grey
; spoke node reads 0.286/0.254/0.285 -- so it is used for RELATIONSHIPS only:
; collars lighter and warmer than the barrel, tram banded red on white, core
; collar rings red-orange. Never for absolutes.

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://scripts/render_shot.gd" id="1"]

